Given a polynomial p(x) = x^6 + ax + b, and knowing that p(x) is divisible by x^2 - 2x - 1, we can use the fact that if p(x) is divisible by a quadratic polynomial q(x), then p(x) must satisfy the roots of q(x). The roots of x^2 - 2x - 1 are x = 1 + \sqrt{2} and x = 1 - \sqrt{2}. So, we get p(1 + \sqrt{2}) = 0 and p(1 - \sqrt{2}) = 0. Let's substitute these roots into the polynomial and solve for a and b. \begin{align*}
p(1 + \sqrt{2}) &= (1 + \sqrt{2})^6 + a(1 + \sqrt{2}) + b = 0, \\
p(1 - \sqrt{2}) &= (1 - \sqrt{2})^6 + a(1 - \sqrt{2}) + b = 0.
\end{align*} Both expressions simplify to the same equation, since (1 + \sqrt{2})^6 = (1 - \sqrt{2})^6. Let's expand the equations first: \begin{align*}
(1 + \sqrt{2})^6 &= (1 + \sqrt{2})^2 (1 + \sqrt{2})^2 (1 + \sqrt{2})^2, \\
(1 - \sqrt{2})^6 &= (1 - \sqrt{2})^2 (1 - \sqrt{2})^2 (1 - \sqrt{2})^2.
\end{align*} We know that (1 + \sqrt{2})^2 = 3 + 2\sqrt{2} and (1 - \sqrt{2})^2 = 3 - 2\sqrt{2}. So, \begin{align*}
(1 + \sqrt{2})^6 &= (3 + 2\sqrt{2})^3 = (3 + 2\sqrt{2})(3 + 2\sqrt{2})(3 + 2\sqrt{2}), \\
(1 - \sqrt{2})^6 &= (3 - 2\sqrt{2})^3 = (3 - 2\sqrt{2})(3 - 2\sqrt{2})(3 - 2\sqrt{2}).
\end{align*} Expanding these, we get \begin{align*}
(3 + 2\sqrt{2})^3 &= (7 + 12\sqrt{2})(3 + 2\sqrt{2}) = (21 + 14\sqrt{2} + 36\sqrt{2} + 48) = 69 + 50\sqrt{2}, \\
(3 - 2\sqrt{2})^3 &= (7 - 12\sqrt{2})(3 - 2\sqrt{2}) = (21 - 14\sqrt{2} - 36\sqrt{2} + 48) = 69 - 50\sqrt{2}.
\end{align*} So, we have \begin{align*}
69 + 50\sqrt{2} + a(1 + \sqrt{2}) + b &= 0, \\
69 - 50\sqrt{2} + a(1 - \sqrt{2}) + b &= 0.
\end{align*} Separating the rational and irrational parts, we get two systems of equations: \begin{cases} 69 + a + b = 0, \\ 50 + a = 0, \end{cases} and \begin{cases} 69 + a + b = 0, \\ -50 - a = 0. \end{cases} Both systems simplify to the same equations, so we get a = -50 and b = -19. So, a + b = -50 - 19 = -69. </think> <think> So, $a + b = -69.$ </think> </think> <think>answer> -69 </think> </think>

